<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Suppressions for the dependency vulnerability scan (mvn -P security-check verify; issue #280).
  This file ships with no entries, and nothing goes into it without the maintainer's approval:
  a scan result is either a fix or a reasoned false positive, and which is a decision, not a
  default.

  THE RULE, enforced by the file itself and not by anyone remembering it: every <suppress>
  carries an `until` attribute, a date with a trailing Z, and the suppression lapses on that
  date. On and after it the plugin no longer applies the suppression (its own schema says so),
  the finding comes back, and the build fails again on a CVSS of 7 or more. A date written only
  in a comment or in <notes> would be a promise with nothing behind it: nothing would stop the
  suppression on that day, and the rule would be a sentence nobody reads then.

  The plugin's schema makes `until` OPTIONAL, so this file cannot make it mandatory: an entry
  without it never lapses, and nothing rejects it. Keep it on every entry, and review one
  without it as a defect. The attribute goes on <suppress>, never only in <notes>:

    <suppress until="2027-06-01Z">
      <notes><![CDATA[
        The CVE, the jar, the CVSS the scan reported, why this is a false positive or an
        accepted risk, and the evidence: a link to the record, or the code path that shows the
        vulnerable code is not reachable from here. Say what makes the date the right one.
      ]]></notes>
      <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/group\.id/artifact-id@.*$</packageUrl>
      <cve>CVE-0000-00000</cve>
    </suppress>

  Scope an entry as narrowly as the finding allows: the jar and the CVE, never a whole jar
  or a whole class of findings.
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.4.xsd">
</suppressions>
